@startuml corpus-prefetch-sequence
' PrefetchingReader: one producer thread, a bounded crossbeam channel of
' capacity B batches, and the three shutdown paths (Done, Error, early Drop).
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/corpus-prefetch-sequence.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 2 in docs/components/corpus/streaming.md, section "Prefetching: decoupling I-O from work".
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam sequence {
  ParticipantFontColor white
  ParticipantBorderColor #263238
  ActorFontColor #263238
  LifeLineBorderColor #90A4AE
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  GroupBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  GroupBorderColor #546E7A
  DividerBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  BoxLineColor #546E7A
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #E3F2FD
  BorderColor #1565C0
}
title PrefetchingReader — producer thread, bounded channel, and the three exits

participant "**consumer**\n(training loop)" as C #455A64
participant "**bounded channel**\ncrossbeam::bounded(B)\ncapacity = B batches" as Q #00838F
participant "**producer thread**\nproducer_loop(reader, tx, stop, S)" as P #546E7A
participant "**stop_signal**\nArc<AtomicBool>" as F #90A4AE

== construction ==
C -> P : thread::spawn(producer_loop)
note over P #ECEFF1
  B = effective_buffer_batches()
  S = config.batch_size (default 10 000)
end note

== steady state — the producer runs ahead ==
loop until reader.sentences() is exhausted
  P -> P : batch.push(sentence)
  alt batch.len() >= S
    P -> Q : send(Batch(Vec<String>))
    note right of Q
      **Back-pressure.** send() **blocks** while the
      channel holds B batches, so the producer can
      never run more than B·S sentences ahead.
      Resident sentences ≤ (B + 2)·S.
    end note
  end
  P -> F : load(Acquire)
  F --> P : stop?  ✗ keep going
end

C -> Q : recv()
Q --> C : Batch(Vec<String>)
C -> C : batch.par_iter().for_each(...)

== normal completion ==
P -> Q : send(Batch(final partial batch))
P -> Q : send(Done)
Q --> C : Done ⇒ exhausted = true ⇒ Iterator returns None

== producer panic ==
P -> P : catch_unwind traps the panic
P -> Q : send(Error(msg))
Q --> C : Error ⇒ log::error! ⇒ iteration ends (no unwind in the consumer)

== early drop / stop() ==
C -> F : store(true, Release)
C -> Q : drain with try_recv() to unblock a blocked send
P -> F : load(Acquire) ⇒ ✓ stop
P -> P : break out of the loop
C -> P : handle.join()
note over C, P
  Drop runs this sequence automatically. Draining **before**
  joining is what prevents the classic deadlock: a producer
  parked inside a blocking send() on a full channel can only
  observe the stop flag after a receive frees a slot.
end note
@enduml
